# fig_headroom_ladder

**Claim carried:** C2: where the gain over PPI++ is zero, small or large, per dataset.

**Shows:** per dataset at its headline n, mean interval width of each step of the ladder divided by PPI++ width (same draws), with the 95% CI of the paired ratio; hollow marker = the method misses the pre-registered win rule (coverage) in that cell; 'n/a' = step not defined for that dataset; a triangle at the top edge = ratio above the plotted range (value printed; the labels-only interval is 1.2-2.9 times PPI++). Floor = the model-based predicted ratio of the standard deviations (bar; no interval, no coverage) and, beside it, the same floor in measured-width units (hollow diamond; headline.csv `floor_ratio_measured`); neither is a bound, both are estimates within a class of calibrators, and neither is a method. 'PPI++ unclipped' is PPI++ with the same estimated tuning but without the package's clip of it to [0, 1] (`ppi-python` 0.2.3), on the same draws, as a ratio to the package PPI++.

**How to read:** grey band = pre-fixed no-gain margin [0.98, 1.02], dashed = gain threshold 0.95 (the CI upper bound must lie below it); the large marker is the headline arm 'Selected' and 'Selected + X' for T2. below the line at 1 = shorter than PPI++; a CI that contains 1 = no detectable difference; hollow = width is not credited. A double dagger above a headline arm marks a cell in which neither the package PPI++ nor the unclipped PPI++ passes the coverage rule (5 cells: alphafold T1; alphafold T2; census income T1; census income T2; forest T1), so the reference line is itself an undercovering interval there.

